{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"47a32f8834cd94f7a2d849e22413e66c3e6b391e057b74d47ce1608235a01263","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_6133908ae33ea3edcd5a084f0acf1f54c13c4ea220b2ee8fb8b2f5c6bcc2d99d","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a method for learning optimization algorithms for high-dimensional stochastic problems, specifically for training shallow neural networks. The primary contribution is an extension of the Learning to Optimize framework that incorporates permutation invariance to handle the high dimensionality of neural network parameters. The paper demonstrates that the learned algorithm generalizes to unseen datasets and architectures, outperforming standard hand-engineered optimizers.","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","paper_identity_sha256":"1a5332bd50cc8564ff0e13b97d47478a0a7ebabf71654e27faa6dc2ccbbaa20c","prompt_identity_sha256":"14b471a67de57ac108e7024a0f87303169c9e9459d2decfc7c696e646511dc51","raw_response_sha256":"7c33ee4252f49c114865c0ea428493dfbf583f5265f51bdde057dc2a247f7e17","request_identity_sha256":"c16a1949470ca784cdd3889baeca4206ce5896f24df7355baf835b0ad8583ae7","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"6a550b1f09993ce9bfdd2db2c653b409a1eb1f2a0435327cac57b08548a3e881","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-003","SRC-005"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a fundamentally new problem formulation but rather extends the existing 'Learning to Optimize' framework to a new domain: high-dimensional stochastic optimization for neural networks. The authors explicitly state they build on the method proposed by Li & Malik (2016) and develop an extension suited for this setting. The problem of learning task-independent optimization algorithms is already established in the cited prior work. The novelty lies in the application of this framework to a challenging, high-dimensional setting rather than the definition of a new problem or task.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-019","SRC-026","SRC-028","SRC-030"],"review_text":"The methodology introduces a specific extension to Guided Policy Search (GPS) to handle high-dimensional state spaces. The key algorithmic contribution is the imposition of permutation invariance priors, which decompose the optimization problem into independent subproblems for each coordinate group (e.g., weight matrices). This allows the use of Linear Quadratic Gaussian (LQG) regulators on smaller subspaces, making the policy search computationally feasible for neural network training. This is a non-trivial modification of the base RL algorithm, tailored to the structural properties of neural network optimization.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-037","SRC-039","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper provides empirical insights into the generalization capabilities of learned optimization algorithms. It demonstrates that an algorithm trained on MNIST can generalize to datasets with very different statistics (TFD, CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100) and to different neural network architectures. The analysis highlights that the learned algorithm is robust to changes in stochasticity (mini-batch size) and can recover from initial oscillations, suggesting it learns to detect poor performance and adjust its strategy. These observations offer new understanding of how meta-learned optimizers interact with the geometry of neural network loss surfaces.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-034","SRC-036","SRC-038"],"review_text":"The experimental setup is designed to test the generalization of the learned optimizer across different datasets, architectures, and stochasticity levels. The paper uses a single training objective (MNIST) and evaluates on three distinct datasets (TFD, CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100) with varying network sizes and mini-batch sizes. This setup is meaningful for assessing the robustness and transferability of the learned algorithm, going beyond simple in-distribution performance. The comparison includes both hand-engineered optimizers and a learned optimizer from a prior method (Andrychowicz et al., 2016), providing a comprehensive benchmark.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-019","SRC-028","SRC-030"],"review_text":"The paper combines Guided Policy Search (a reinforcement learning method) with structural priors (permutation invariance) specific to neural network optimization. The rationale for this combination is well-articulated: the high dimensionality of neural network parameters makes standard RL intractable, and the permutation invariance prior allows the problem to be decomposed into smaller, solvable subproblems. This synergy between the RL framework and the structural properties of the optimization task is a creative and effective combination that enables the application of learned optimizers to a new class of problems.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-039","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper does not primarily focus on negative results or critical assessments of existing methods. While it notes that some hand-engineered algorithms (e.g., conjugate gradient) and the prior learned method (L2LBGDBGD) struggle with increased stochasticity or diverge on certain datasets, these observations are incidental to the main goal of demonstrating the superiority of the proposed method. The paper does not present a systematic failure analysis or a critical evaluation of the limitations of the learning-to-optimize paradigm itself.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"1024562ec166b826de807cc23d0df5e0c4fc950ca946f47cb666c253f735dd34","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","paper_ordinal":97,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"7c33ee4252f49c114865c0ea428493dfbf583f5265f51bdde057dc2a247f7e17","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BkM27IxR-","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"c2e667b26598607219a2ecdb7edd42ca053055b006650f1b0202643534c06d89","request_identity_sha256":"c16a1949470ca784cdd3889baeca4206ce5896f24df7355baf835b0ad8583ae7","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":1192,"prompt_tokens":18596,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19788},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"c16a1949470ca784cdd3889baeca4206ce5896f24df7355baf835b0ad8583ae7","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}